1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,580
Twelve forty-seven p.m. Thirty-five thousand feet above South Florida.

2
00:00:06,517 --> 00:00:11,759
The crew of a business jet radios air traffic control: light clear ice,

3
00:00:11,809 --> 00:00:14,667
moderate turbulence. They ask to climb.

4
00:00:15,617 --> 00:00:18,763
Within about a minute, the jet is gone from the sky.

5
00:00:18,813 --> 00:00:23,127
Investigators say it began to break apart before it reached the ground.

6
00:00:24,083 --> 00:00:27,393
So what have they found so far, and what don't they know yet?

7
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:37,896
It was Sunday, the first of November. A Learjet 60, registration N722BD,

8
00:00:37,946 --> 00:00:43,400
was flying from Austin, Texas, to North Eleuthera in the Bahamas.

9
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:47,356
On board: two pilots, and seven passengers.

10
00:00:47,406 --> 00:00:52,545
A family from Austin with their two young children, the children's nanny,

11
00:00:52,595 --> 00:00:54,180
and two family friends.

12
00:00:55,117 --> 00:01:00,334
The jet took off from Austin-Bergstrom at nine thirty-seven a.m. local time,

13
00:01:00,384 --> 00:01:02,067
ten thirty-seven Eastern.

14
00:01:03,017 --> 00:01:05,740
According to Flightradar24 tracking data,

15
00:01:05,790 --> 00:01:09,393
it climbed to forty-one thousand feet and headed east,

16
00:01:09,443 --> 00:01:11,287
out over the Gulf of Mexico.

17
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:16,412
For about two hours, everything about the flight appeared routine.

18
00:01:16,462 --> 00:01:17,752
At cruising altitude,

19
00:01:17,802 --> 00:01:21,517
a business jet like this is flown largely by the autopilot,

20
00:01:21,567 --> 00:01:26,240
while the two pilots monitor the systems, the weather ahead and the radio.

21
00:01:27,183 --> 00:01:32,013
Their destination was a small island airport on the Atlantic side of the Bahamas.

22
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:38,529
The Learjet name goes back to the 1960s, when Bill Lear set out to build a fast,

23
00:01:38,579 --> 00:01:40,780
compact jet for business travel.

24
00:01:41,717 --> 00:01:47,258
The Learjet 60 is a mid-size business jet, built by Bombardier in Wichita,

25
00:01:47,308 --> 00:01:49,827
Kansas, and in service since 1993.

26
00:01:50,783 --> 00:01:54,076
Two rear-mounted engines, room for up to eight passengers,

27
00:01:54,126 --> 00:01:57,533
and a cruising speed of around four hundred and fifty knots.

28
00:01:58,483 --> 00:02:02,828
This one was built in 2004 and operated by Aurora Aviation,

29
00:02:02,878 --> 00:02:04,913
doing business as Upper Jet.

30
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:10,034
As the jet crossed the Florida coast near Naples, at about twelve thirty-five,

31
00:02:10,084 --> 00:02:14,920
the tracking data show it descending from forty-one thousand to thirty-five thousand feet.

32
00:02:15,883 --> 00:02:17,977
Ahead, the weather was changing:

33
00:02:18,027 --> 00:02:22,130
thunderstorms were reported over South Florida that afternoon,

34
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:24,273
with strong gusts on the ground.

35
00:02:25,217 --> 00:02:29,209
The NTSB has said the weather conditions are, in its words,

36
00:02:29,259 --> 00:02:31,607
a major focus of the investigation.

37
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:36,607
At twelve forty-seven, at about thirty-five thousand feet,

38
00:02:36,657 --> 00:02:40,997
the crew contacted air traffic control. According to the NTSB,

39
00:02:41,047 --> 00:02:44,820
they reported light clear ice and moderate turbulence.

40
00:02:45,783 --> 00:02:49,573
Their altitude was moving up and down by around seven hundred feet.

41
00:02:50,517 --> 00:02:55,507
They asked to climb to thirty-seven thousand feet. Controllers approved.

42
00:02:56,450 --> 00:02:58,245
To understand what happened next,

43
00:02:58,295 --> 00:03:01,040
we need to look at what the crew was dealing with.

44
00:03:01,983 --> 00:03:06,718
A single thunderstorm can be ten miles wide and fifty thousand feet tall,

45
00:03:06,768 --> 00:03:10,453
according to NOAA, well above where business jets cruise.

46
00:03:11,417 --> 00:03:16,310
Inside and around them, powerful updrafts and downdrafts create turbulence,

47
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:21,847
which is why the FAA advises pilots to avoid severe storms by at least twenty miles.

48
00:03:22,783 --> 00:03:24,513
Ice is the second hazard.

49
00:03:24,563 --> 00:03:30,493
Clear ice forms when supercooled water droplets freeze on contact with the aircraft.

50
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:34,564
It can change the shape of wings and tail surfaces,

51
00:03:34,614 --> 00:03:37,480
which is why jets carry ice protection systems.

52
00:03:38,417 --> 00:03:42,429
Every aircraft also has a maximum speed it is allowed to fly,

53
00:03:42,479 --> 00:03:47,156
a limit set during certification, with a margin built in. Go beyond it,

54
00:03:47,206 --> 00:03:49,887
and a loud warning sounds in the cockpit.

55
00:03:50,850 --> 00:03:53,810
Back to twelve forty-seven. The climb was approved,

56
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:56,880
and the tracking data show the jet climbing briefly.

57
00:03:57,817 --> 00:03:59,739
Then, according to the NTSB,

58
00:03:59,789 --> 00:04:05,797
less than thirty seconds later a transmission from the aircraft announced the descent:

59
00:04:05,847 --> 00:04:08,967
We are in an overspeed condition, descending.

60
00:04:09,917 --> 00:04:15,658
Then a mayday. Flightradar24 data show the jet entering a steep descent,

61
00:04:15,708 --> 00:04:17,347
turning to the right.

62
00:04:18,283 --> 00:04:19,481
According to FAA data,

63
00:04:19,531 --> 00:04:26,799
the jet dropped from about thirty-five thousand two hundred and fifty feet to around eight thousand seven hundred and fifty feet,

64
00:04:26,849 --> 00:04:28,273
in a little over a minute.

65
00:04:29,217 --> 00:04:33,748
That was the last altitude the tracking data captured. Moments later,

66
00:04:33,798 --> 00:04:35,807
the aircraft struck the ground.

67
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:43,198
Through all of it, the pilots kept talking to air traffic control:

68
00:04:43,248 --> 00:04:47,680
reporting the conditions, announcing the descent, declaring the emergency.

69
00:04:48,617 --> 00:04:52,372
The aircraft came down in Greenacres, in Palm Beach County,

70
00:04:52,422 --> 00:04:54,887
near a busy road called Military Trail.

71
00:04:55,850 --> 00:05:01,080
All nine people on board were killed. A person in a nearby car was injured.

72
00:05:03,017 --> 00:05:07,949
Hundreds of residents of a neighbouring mobile home community had to leave their homes,

73
00:05:07,999 --> 00:05:10,527
and a nearby high school closed the next day.

74
00:05:11,483 --> 00:05:14,524
Within hours, an NTSB team was on scene.

75
00:05:14,574 --> 00:05:19,856
Wreckage was spread across about half a mile of homes and businesses,

76
00:05:19,906 --> 00:05:21,633
some of it on rooftops.

77
00:05:22,583 --> 00:05:27,490
Investigators asked residents not to keep or move anything they found,

78
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:28,693
but to report it.

79
00:05:29,650 --> 00:05:35,200
Their first task on any accident scene is to document everything before it is moved:

80
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:40,200
where each piece lies, how it is damaged, how far it is from the main site.

81
00:05:41,150 --> 00:05:44,120
Teams cleared a mobile home community, businesses,

82
00:05:44,170 --> 00:05:47,260
a storage facility and the grounds of a high school.

83
00:05:48,217 --> 00:05:51,690
By the eighth of November, most of the on-scene work was done,

84
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:54,247
apart from excavating the impact site itself.

85
00:05:55,183 --> 00:05:59,023
Then came the finding that changed the picture. The left elevator,

86
00:05:59,073 --> 00:06:03,620
the movable part of the tail that controls whether the nose points up or down,

87
00:06:03,670 --> 00:06:05,093
was not at the main site.

88
00:06:06,050 --> 00:06:07,807
It was found in three pieces,

89
00:06:07,857 --> 00:06:11,360
between one point three and three point eight miles away.

90
00:06:12,317 --> 00:06:18,081
NTSB board member Tom Chapman said that knowing it was lost is a very important piece of evidence,

91
00:06:18,131 --> 00:06:22,827
and that at least some important pieces of the aircraft separated before impact.

92
00:06:23,783 --> 00:06:27,933
What that means for the cause, he added, investigators don't know yet.

93
00:06:28,883 --> 00:06:32,810
Investigators recovered parts of both wings, both engines,

94
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:34,799
the flight deck and the tail.

95
00:06:34,849 --> 00:06:38,913
Some of them went to the NTSB laboratory in Washington, D.C.

96
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:43,750
What investigators have not said yet: why the elevator separated,

97
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:47,640
whether the weather played a role, and what caused the accident.

98
00:06:48,583 --> 00:06:51,893
Until they do, anything else is speculation.

99
00:06:52,850 --> 00:06:56,492
One more piece is now in the lab: the cockpit voice recorder,

100
00:06:56,542 --> 00:06:59,760
recovered at the impact site on the ninth of November.

101
00:07:00,717 --> 00:07:04,552
Despite the nickname black box, it is bright orange.

102
00:07:04,602 --> 00:07:10,455
It records the pilots' voices, radio calls and sounds in the cockpit: warnings,

103
00:07:10,505 --> 00:07:11,427
engine noise.

104
00:07:12,383 --> 00:07:17,241
The audio is never made public. Only a written transcript is released,

105
00:07:17,291 --> 00:07:19,133
later in the investigation.

106
00:07:20,083 --> 00:07:24,288
At the NTSB laboratory, specialists first download the recording.

107
00:07:24,338 --> 00:07:28,542
Then a group of experts listens to it together, often many times,

108
00:07:28,592 --> 00:07:33,713
and writes down every word and every relevant sound, matched to the exact time.

109
00:07:34,650 --> 00:07:38,280
That recording may help answer the questions that are still open.

110
00:07:39,217 --> 00:07:42,321
A preliminary report is expected within weeks.

111
00:07:42,371 --> 00:07:47,327
The NTSB will publish it with the facts gathered so far, without a cause.

112
00:07:48,283 --> 00:07:51,910
These reports usually describe the flight path, the weather,

113
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:54,913
the crew's experience and the aircraft's history.

114
00:07:55,850 --> 00:08:02,641
The full investigation usually takes twelve to twenty-four months and ends with a final report and a probable cause.

115
00:08:02,691 --> 00:08:05,000
We'll cover each step when it comes out.

116
00:08:05,950 --> 00:08:10,353
We remember the nine people who lost their lives: Jeffrey Gregory,

117
00:08:10,403 --> 00:08:15,144
Nathalia Gadelha Filgueiras, Adam Robinson, Helen Sharp, Emma Robinson,

118
00:08:15,194 --> 00:08:20,340
Charles Robinson, Ana Maria Ramirez Cetina, Rowan Fehr Toews and Rachel Reed.

119
00:08:22,283 --> 00:08:25,378
Our thoughts are with their families, their friends,

120
00:08:25,428 --> 00:08:27,313
and the community in Greenacres.

121
00:08:29,250 --> 00:08:33,234
If you want to know what the preliminary report reveals, subscribe.

122
00:08:33,284 --> 00:08:35,040
We'll be back, with the facts.
